@@s001 Dijkstra: shortest paths with non-negative weights
@@s002 ora:node just fixed,dato:provisional distance,ok:final distance
@@s003 Weights on the edges (costs ≥ 0). Provisional distances: <code>d(s) = 0</code>, all the others ∞.
@@s004 Fix <b>s</b> (final distance 0). Relax its neighbours: s→a costs 2, so d(a) = 2; s→b costs 5, so d(b) = 5.
@@s005 Fix the minimum of the queue: <b>a</b> (2). Relax: a→b costs 1, so 2 + 1 = 3 &lt; 5: <b>d(b) = 3</b>, parent a. a→c costs 4: d(c) = 6.
@@s006 Fix <b>b</b> (3). Relax b→c: 3 + 1 = 4 &lt; 6: <b>d(c) = 4</b>.
@@s007 Fix <b>c</b> (4). No neighbour to improve. All distances are now final: <b>s 0, a 2, b 3, c 4</b>. The shortest-path tree is read from the parents.
@@s008 Dijkstra: shortest paths with non-negative weights
@@s009 Dijkstra's algorithm finds the minimum cost from one source to all the other nodes, when all the weights are ≥ 0. It is greedy: at each step it makes final the smallest distance.
@@s010 The problem and the idea
@@s011 The problem: edges with <b>weight</b> (cost) <code>ℓ(u, v) ≥ 0</code>, and a source <code>s</code>. I want the minimum cost from <code>s</code> to every node.
@@s012 The idea: each node keeps a <b>provisional distance</b> <code>d(v)</code>, at the start <code>0</code> for <code>s</code> and <code>∞</code> for the others. At each step you take the node with the smallest <code>d</code> among those not yet fixed: that distance becomes <b>final</b>.
@@s013 Dijkstra: fix the minimum, update the neighbours
@@s014 Relaxation
@@s015 The step that updates the distances is called <b>relaxation</b>. For each edge <code>u → v</code> with weight <code>ℓ</code>:
@@s016 <code>if d(u) + ℓ &lt; d(v) then d(v) = d(u) + ℓ and parent(v) = u</code>
@@s017 It means: "going through <code>u</code> I reach <code>v</code> with cost <code>d(u) + ℓ</code>; if that is better than what I already know, I keep it."
@@s018 Why it works, and why the weights must be ≥ 0
@@s019 When you fix the node <code>u</code> with distance <code>d(u)</code>, no path that passes through nodes not yet fixed can cost less: every edge adds a cost <code>≥ 0</code>, so a path with more edges does not become cheaper. With a negative weight this guarantee fails: a path that looks more expensive can become cheap further on.
@@s020 Negative weights: not Dijkstra
@@s021 With negative weights Dijkstra gives wrong results, even when there are no negative cycles. For that case use Bellman-Ford (the dynamic programming page).
@@s022 Cost and priority queue
@@s023 It needs: <code>n</code> insertions, <code>n</code> extractions of the minimum and at most <code>m</code> decrease-key operations. With a <b>binary heap</b> (priority queue) the cost is <code>O(m log n)</code>. With an unsorted array <code>O(n²)</code>, good for dense graphs. With a Fibonacci heap <code>O(m + n log n)</code>, more theoretical.
@@s024 The code, side by side
@@s025 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes you need, is on the side-by-side page.
@@s026 <a href="../codice/dijkstra.html"><b>→ Java code for this topic</b></a>
